$frontmatter {
  title: Token Counting
  description: Count provider-side input tokens before generation and use counts for budget reservation.
  tags = ["tokens", "budget", "adapters"]
}

"#": Token Counting

"intro" = ```markdown
Token counting is a provider request that asks the configured adapter how many input tokens a
turn would use. It does not send a generation request, does not commit to a `Session`, and does
not reserve budget by itself.
```

"toc".$toc = true

@ "setup" {
  "##": Setup

  "body" = ```markdown
Attach a token counter to `Lutum` with `with_token_counter`. The same adapter can usually be used
as both the generation adapter and the counter:
```

  "rust-example" = ```rust
use std::sync::Arc;
use lutum::{Lutum, ModelName, SharedPoolBudgetManager, SharedPoolBudgetOptions};
use lutum_openai::OpenAiAdapter;

let adapter = Arc::new(
    OpenAiAdapter::new(std::env::var("OPENAI_API_KEY")?)
        .with_default_model(ModelName::new("gpt-5.4-nano")?)
);

let llm = Lutum::new(
    adapter.clone(),
    SharedPoolBudgetManager::new(SharedPoolBudgetOptions::default()),
)
.with_token_counter(adapter);
```

  "body-2" = ```markdown
`ClaudeAdapter` implements counting with Anthropic's Messages count-tokens endpoint.
`OpenAiAdapter` implements counting for OpenAI Responses API requests that expose
`/responses/input_tokens`.
```

}

@ "same-builder" {
  "##": Count Then Collect With The Same Builder

  "body" = ```markdown
`count_tokens()` borrows the builder. You can count, inspect the result, and then use the exact
same builder for generation:
```

  "rust-example" = ```rust
use lutum::Session;

let mut session = Session::new();
session.push_system("Answer concisely.");
session.push_user("Explain provider-side token counting.");

let turn = session.text_turn(&llm).max_output_tokens(64);

let count = turn.count_tokens().await?;
if let Some(count) = count {
    println!("input tokens: {}", count.input_tokens);
}

let result = turn.collect().await?;
println!("{}", result.assistant_text());
```

  "body-2" = ```markdown
Counting a `Session` turn previews the current `ModelInput`. It does not auto-commit, does not
strip ephemeral session items, and does not mutate the transcript. The later `collect()` call is
the operation that starts generation and commits the completed turn.
```

}

@ "model-input" {
  "##": Count A ModelInput

  "body" = ```markdown
For code that manages `ModelInput` directly, use the same turn builder from `Lutum`:
```

  "rust-example" = ```rust
use lutum::{InputMessageRole, ModelInput, ModelInputItem};

let input = ModelInput::from_items(vec![
    ModelInputItem::text(InputMessageRole::User, "Summarize this input."),
]);

let turn = llm.text_turn(input).max_output_tokens(32);

let count = turn.count_tokens().await?;
let result = turn.collect().await?;
```

}

@ "unsupported" {
  "##": Unsupported Surfaces

  "body" = ```markdown
`count_tokens()` returns `Result<Option<TokenCount>, AgentError>`.

| Return | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `Ok(Some(count))` | The provider returned an exact input token count |
| `Ok(None)` | No counter is attached, or this adapter surface does not support exact counting |
| `Err(err)` | The count request failed before generation |

OpenAI Chat Completions and legacy Completions currently return `Ok(None)`.
OpenRouter and Ollama-compatible endpoints are not enabled in the smoke config for exact
counting unless they expose the same count endpoints.
```

}

@ "budget" {
  "##": Budget Reservation

  "body" = ```markdown
When a token counter is attached, normal `.start()`, `.stream()`, and `.collect()` paths count
before reserving budget. A successful count replaces only the input-token part of the reservation:

| Field | Reservation value |
|---|---|
| `input_tokens` | Exact provider count |
| `output_tokens` | Existing output/non-input estimate, at least the configured `max_output_tokens` when set |
| `total_tokens` | Exact input count plus the preserved output/non-input estimate |
| `cost_micros_usd` | Existing estimate |

Counting itself is not generation usage. Cost accounting for completed generations still comes
from provider usage events or usage recovery, not from the count request.
```

}
